SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

An SO-101 leader gripper locked to the operator's controller grip pose, and the
whole of the shipped scene. It shows app.py's grip calibration -- whether the
tool sits in the hand the way a hand holds one -- but not cpp/frames.hpp, whose
constants place the eye pose through the same transform and so cancel.

Opaque, which makes draw order free. A scene that drops the alpha back must
include this LAST, since mjv_updateScene emits in geom-id order. See
README.md#scene-assets.

Provenance: all four meshes are TheRobotStudio/SO-ARM100 at commit
fda892cba81032c46c40976a48c9ceadbf40a9ca, Apache-2.0. Three are leader-specific
print parts; the fourth is the STS3215 servo, shared with the follower. Fetched
by scripts/fetch-so-arm.sh, not vendored; only this wrapper is tracked.

The STLs are not in print orientation -- they sit on a shared CAD datum, which
is why the transforms below are derived rather than tuned:

    Handle_SO101.stl        z[ 36.703,  96.768]
    Trigger_SO101.stl       z[-26.000,   6.000]
    Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl    x[-35.200,  30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000]
    wrist_roll_follower...  x[-35.000,  30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000]  <- SAME DATUM

scale="0.001": SO-ARM STLs are in MILLIMETRES. Getting it wrong puts the camera
inside a 65 m solid, which reads as "passthrough broke".
-->
<mujoco model="so101 leader gripper ghost">
  <asset>
    <!-- MuJoCo resolves an included file's assets relative to THAT file, which
         is why these names carry no prefix and why this fragment lives beside
         the STLs it names. -->
    <mesh name="leader_wrist_roll" file="Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="leader_trigger" file="Trigger_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="leader_handle" file="Handle_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <!-- No `scale`, and not an oversight: this is Menagerie's asset, authored
         in metres, where the three above are SO-ARM print STLs in millimetres.
         scale="0.001" here shrinks the servo to 45 microns and it vanishes
         rather than erroring. -->
    <mesh name="leader_motor" file="STS3215_03a.stl"/>
    <!-- Opaque; see the header for what the alpha buys. -->
    <material name="leader_ghost" rgba="0.55 0.80 1.00 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- A MOCAP BODY, so MuJoCo owns the compile-time mesh_pos/mesh_quat
         composition that decor geoms would have to reproduce by hand. `pos` is
         where the ghost waits for its first valid controller sample, clear of
         the scene so "not moved yet" reads differently from "broken". -->
    <body name="leader_ghost" mocap="true" pos="0.12 0.16 0.22"
          quat="0.5 0.5 -0.5 -0.5">
      <!-- DERIVED, NOT TUNED. so101.xml puts the follower's fixed-jaw shank at
           pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0" in the `gripper` body, and
           Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl is that same part on the same datum with the
           fixed jaw amputated. So this slot is Menagerie's, verbatim. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_wrist_roll" type="mesh" mesh="leader_wrist_roll"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>

      <!-- The servo. Without it wrist_roll's C-shaped bracket leaves an open
           notch and the assembly reads as broken. From the URDF's second
           gripper_link visual; the follower's servo is correct here because
           the two share the hardware. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_motor" type="mesh" mesh="leader_motor"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0.0077 0.0001 -0.0234"
            quat="0.70711 -0.70711 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>

      <!-- DERIVED, not tuned: the handle is on the same CAD datum as the
           shank, so it takes the SAME transform. The evidence is the assembled
           gap -- 0.3 mm shank-to-handle, 2.1 mm to the pivoting trigger --
           which tests/test_ghost.py asserts, so a mesh refresh that broke the
           datum fails rather than drifts. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_handle" type="mesh" mesh="leader_handle"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The trigger, on its own mocap channel because it articulates. A second
         mocap body rather than a hinge joint: a jointed child of a mocap body
         compiles and honours a written qpos, but mj_step integrates gravity
         into it (measured: 0.06 rad over 50 steps).

         `pos`/`quat` match leader_ghost above -- app.py rotates this body about
         a point rather than placing its origin there, so the hinge lives in
         exactly one place. -->
    <body name="leader_ghost_jaw" mocap="true" pos="0.12 0.16 0.22"
          quat="0.5 0.5 -0.5 -0.5">
      <!-- Straight from upstream. The trigger occupies the follower's
           `moving_jaw_so101_v1` slot: so101_new_calib.urdf gives the `gripper`
           joint origin as xyz="0.0202 0.0188 -0.0234" rpy="1.5708 0 0",
           composed here with that body's own visual geom pos="0 0 0.0189".
           This is the joint's zero pose; app.py swings it from there. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_trigger" type="mesh" mesh="leader_trigger"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0.0202 -0.0001 -0.0234" quat="1 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
